-- title:
--   Reduction of Uₚ preserves the Néron special fibre torus
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime and $M$ a nonzero natural number with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$.
--
--   **Coefficient data.** $L$ is a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ for $\{p\}$, and $\zeta \in L$ is a primitive $p$-th root of unity. $K$ is an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ which by `hK` equals [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), that is, the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise images of the elements of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ inside $\mathbb{Q}((q))$. $A$ is a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in its maximal ideal (`hAp`) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ (`hζA`), and $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with the tower $A \to L \to K$. Finally $j \in K$ has Laurent expansion the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) of the modular function $j$ (`hj`), and $j \neq 0$. The $A$-scheme at issue is the two-chart model [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, which is assumed proper; `ε` is a section of it over $\operatorname{Spec} A$.
--
--   **Special fibre.** $k$ is an algebraically closed $A$-algebra of characteristic $p$. $C_1, C_2$ are $k$-schemes whose structure morphisms $c_1, c_2$ are proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral, equipped with closed immersions $i_1, i_2$ into the base change of the two-chart model to $k$ (that is, into $\mathrm{pullback}$ of `modelTo` along $\operatorname{Spec} k \to \operatorname{Spec} A$), compatibly with the structure morphisms. The hypothesis `hcover` says that every point of that base change lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` says that the fibre product of $i_1$ and $i_2$ is reduced; `hn` names its cardinality $n$, and `hn0` requires $n > 0$. Sections $\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2$ of $c_1, c_2$ over $\operatorname{Spec} k$ are given, with `hε₁` asserting that $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ is the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $k$.
--
--   **Relative Picard data.** $D$ is a `RelativePic0Designation` for `modelTo`, i.e. a scheme $D.P$ with a structure morphism `D.toBase` to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a zero section; `hrep` asserts that $D$, together with $\varepsilon$, represents the relative sub-Picard functor cut out by the condition `algEquivZeroCut` (fibrewise algebraic equivalence to zero): there is a rigidified Poincaré bundle on the curve pulled back along `D.toBase`, satisfying the condition, such that any rigidified line bundle over a base $T$ satisfying the condition is induced by a unique morphism $T \to D.\mathrm{toBase}$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, and the zero section pulls the Poincaré bundle back to the unit. `hsm` and `hsep` say that `D.toBase` is smooth and separated. `hreps` is the corresponding representability statement after base change to $k$, for `D.baseChange k`, and `hPk` requires the Poincaré bundle of `hreps` to be isomorphic to the base change of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep.some` along the first projection. $D_1, D_2$ are such designations for $c_1, c_2$, with `hrep₁`, `hrep₂` the corresponding representability assumptions relative to $\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2$. A morphism $\nu_2$ from $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$ to $D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$ over $\operatorname{Spec} k$ is given, characterised by `hν₂`: for every $k$-scheme $t$ and every $T$-point $a$ of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$, the Poincaré bundle of $D_2$ pulled back along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ is isomorphic to the rigidification along $\varepsilon_2$ of the restriction along $i_2$ of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hreps` along $a$.
--
--   **Geometric generic fibre.** Algebra structures $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ in a tower are fixed ($\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`). $M_\eta$ is a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) (a proper smooth integral curve with an identification of its function field with that intermediate field, and a bijection between its closed points and the places), $e_\eta$ an isomorphism from $M_\eta.C$ to the base change of the two-chart model to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ commuting with the structure morphisms (`heη`). The chart hypotheses are: the preimage under $e_\eta$ followed by the first projection of the image of the finite chart is nonempty, and `hMηpin`, which reads off $q$-expansions: for every element $a$ of the finite chart algebra [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.chartAlgFin A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L135), the element of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` corresponding under $M_\eta.\mathrm{ffEquiv}$ to the germ at the generic point of the pullback of $a$ has Laurent series the coefficientwise image, along $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, of the Laurent series of $a$. The hypothesis `hgal` is Galois equivariance: for every $\mathbb{Q}$-automorphism $g$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ fixing the image of $L$ pointwise and all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, x'$ of $M_\eta$, if $x'$ read in the chart equals the $g$-twist of $x$ read in the chart, then the place attached to $x'$ is the image of the place attached to $x$ under [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p)) g`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54).
--
--   **The special fibre as a group.** $G$ is a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9): abelian groups $G.J0s$, $G.JI$, $G.JE$, a subgroup $G.\mathrm{torus} \le G.J0s$, and a surjective homomorphism $G.\mathrm{proj} : G.J0s \to G.JI \times G.JE$ whose kernel is $G.\mathrm{torus}$. Bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` identify $G.J0s$, $G.JI$, $G.JE$ with the $k$-sections of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$, of $D_1.\mathrm{toBase}$ and of $D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$ respectively. The hypotheses `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` say that each of these bijections is additive in the sense that the Poincaré bundle pulled back along the point attached to a sum is isomorphic to the tensor product of the pullbacks along the points attached to the summands. The hypothesis `hproj` says that for every $x \in G.J0s$ the point attached to the first component of $G.\mathrm{proj}\,x$ is `pts x` followed by the morphism `RepresentsRelSubPic.pullbackHom` induced by the closed immersion $i_1$, and the point attached to the second component is `pts x` followed by $\nu_2$.
--
--   **Generic points and the Hecke action.** `gpts` is a bijection from [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186), the degree-zero divisor class group of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, to the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of `D.toBase` over $\operatorname{Spec} A$; `hgadd` says it is additive for the relative group law furnished by `hrep.some`. The map $\varphi$ assigns to each element $t$ of [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16) $= \mathbb{Z}[X_\ell, Y_n]$ (the polynomial ring on primes and naturals) an endomorphism of `D.toBase` over $\operatorname{Spec} A$; `hφmul` says each $\varphi(t)$ is a homomorphism for the relative group law on every base, and `hφpts` says that, for the Hecke module structure [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleOneBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L129) on [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186), one has $\mathrm{gpts}(t \cdot x) = \mathrm{gpts}(x)$ followed by $\varphi(t)$.
--
--   **Abel–Jacobi data.** `hDL` is representability after base change to $L$ for `D.baseChange L`; `ajL` is a morphism from the base-changed curve over $L$ to $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,L).\mathrm{toBase}$ over the base, with `hPL` the compatibility of Poincaré bundles for the base change to $L$, `hajLε` saying that $\varepsilon$ base changed to $L$ followed by `ajL` is the zero section, and `hajL` the Abel–Jacobi property: for every field $K'$, every $K'$-point $t$ of $\operatorname{Spec} L$ and every point $x$ of the curve over $t$, the Poincaré bundle pulled back along $x$ followed by `ajL` is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of the base-changed $\varepsilon$. The morphism `kL` between the base changes to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and to $L$ is compatible with both projections (`hkL₁`, `hkL₂`); `ajbar` is defined by `hajbar` as $e_\eta$ followed by `kL`, `ajL` and the first projection, and lies over the structure morphisms by `hajbar_over`. The $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $\bar\varepsilon$ of $M_\eta$ satisfies `hεbar` (it reads in the chart as the base change of $\varepsilon$) and `hεbar_aj` (it is carried by `ajbar` to the zero section of $D$). The hypothesis `hpts_aj` says that for all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, s$ of $M_\eta$ with $s$ reading in the chart as the base change of $\varepsilon$, there is a degree-zero divisor $Dv$ on `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` equal to the difference of the places attached to $x$ and to $s$, whose class satisfies $\mathrm{gpts}(\,[Dv]\,) = x$ followed by `ajbar`.
--
--   **Igusa side.** $w$ is a [`ModularCurve.IntegralWeightOneForm k M`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L16): a weight-one modular form on $\Gamma_1(M)$ together with an integral $q$-expansion power series whose reduction over $k$ is nonzero. $\mathrm{Mdl}_1$ is a curve model over $k$ of the Igusa function field [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C k M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35), with an isomorphism $e_1 : \mathrm{Mdl}_1.C \cong C_1$ over $k$ (`he₁`), the relevant chart preimage nonempty, and `hgauss₁` the Gauss-type reading of $q$-expansions: for every element $a$ of the finite chart algebra and all power series $x, y$ over $A$ with $y$ having nonzero reduction over $k$, if $a \cdot y = x$ holds in $L((q))$ then the element of the Igusa function field corresponding to the germ of $a$ along $e_1$ followed by $i_1$ and the first projection has Laurent series the quotient of the reductions of $x$ and $y$ in $k((q))$. Further, $\theta_1$ is an isomorphism of abelian groups from $G.JI$ to $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the Igusa function field over $k$, pinned down by `hθpin₁`: whenever the Poincaré bundle pulled back along $\mathrm{ptsI}\,g$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of the divisor of a $k$-point $x$ of $c_1$ tensored with the ideal module of $\varepsilon_1$, then $\theta_1(g)$ is the class of the degree-zero divisor $[x] - [\varepsilon_1]$ read through $e_1$ and $\mathrm{Mdl}_1$. Finally $\mathrm{frobIg}$ is a `SemilinearAut` of the Igusa function field over $k$ (a pair of ring automorphisms compatible with the structure map) acting on Laurent coefficients by $c \mapsto c^p$ (`hfrobIg`).
--
--   **The auxiliary subgroup scheme.** $\mathcal{A}$ is a scheme with structure morphism $a$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a morphism $\iota$ to `D.toBase` over $a$ which is a closed immersion (`h𝒜cl`); $a$ is proper (`h𝒜pr`) and smooth (`h𝒜sm`), all its geometric fibres are connected (`h𝒜conn`), `h𝒜grp` says that the points of $\mathcal{A}$ form a subgroup for the relative group law on every base (closure under the unit, multiplication and inversion, after composing with $\iota$), `h𝒜gen` says that an element $x$ of [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186) lies in the subgroup [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) if and only if $\mathrm{gpts}(x)$ factors through $\iota$, and `h𝒜hecke` says that $\mathcal{A}$ is stable under each $\varphi(t)$.
--
--   **Place data.** $\mathrm{Pl}$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a nonunit in it (`hPl`), $\rho : A \to \mathrm{Pl}$ a ring homomorphism inducing the structure map $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρ`); $O$ is a subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ contained in $\mathrm{Pl}$ (`hO`) with $\rho_O : A \to O$ inducing the same structure map (`hρO`); $\pi_k : \mathrm{Pl} \to k$ is a ring homomorphism with $\pi_k \circ \rho =$ the structure map $A \to k$ (`hAlgk`), and $\pi_k$ is surjective (`hπk`).
--
--   **Conclusion.** Two statements hold, differing only in the ring of integral points used.
--
--   First: for all points $z, z'$ of $D.P$ over $\operatorname{Spec} O$ lying over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\rho_O$, and all $y, y' \in G.J0s$, if `pts y` followed by the first projection of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ along $\operatorname{Spec} k \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\pi_k \circ (O \hookrightarrow \mathrm{Pl})$ followed by $z$ (so $y$ is the reduction of $z$), if $z'$ is $z$ followed by $\varphi(\mathrm{heckeGenOne}\,p)$, the endomorphism attached to the Hecke generator $X_p$, if likewise $y'$ is the reduction of $z'$, and if $G.\mathrm{proj}\,y = 0$, then $G.\mathrm{proj}\,y' = 0$.
--
--   Second: the same assertion with $O$ replaced by $\mathrm{Pl}$ throughout, i.e. for all points $z, z'$ of $D.P$ over $\operatorname{Spec} \mathrm{Pl}$ lying over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\rho$ and all $y, y' \in G.J0s$: if `pts y` followed by the first projection equals $\operatorname{Spec} \pi_k$ followed by $z$, if $z' = z$ followed by $\varphi(\mathrm{heckeGenOne}\,p)$, if `pts y'` followed by the first projection equals $\operatorname{Spec} \pi_k$ followed by $z'$, and if $G.\mathrm{proj}\,y = 0$, then $G.\mathrm{proj}\,y' = 0$.
--
--   Since the kernel of $G.\mathrm{proj}$ is $G.\mathrm{torus}$, each conjunct says that the reduction of the Hecke operator $U_p$ carries reductions of integral points lying in the torus again into the torus.
--
--   This is the torus-stability step in the analysis of the Néron special fibre at $p$ of the Jacobian of $X_1(Mp)$: the endomorphism of the special fibre induced by $U_p$ does not move the toric part out of itself, so that it descends to the abelian quotient $J_I \times J_E$. It is used in the Eichler–Shimura style computation of the matrix of the reduced $U_p$ on the abelian quotient and in the companion statement for the second (Igusa) component of the special fibre.
